{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789230236,"nonce":"lTEpF75NZyveez/Ao1w29g4EUyQ=","title":"InsertValue flattens any single-field struct built from undef; ExtractValue only a scalar one","message":"The Cranelift backend carries a single-field struct as its field when\nthat field is a scalar (`struct_carried_as_its_field`), and by address\notherwise. `ExtractValue` and `translate_type` follow that rule.\n`InsertValue` does not: an insert at index 0 into an undef aggregate\nwith one field returns the inserted value as the struct whatever the\nfield's type. For a pointer field the two sides disagree: the insert\nhands back the pointer, the extract treats it as the struct's address\nand loads eight bytes from wherever it points.\n\nReproducer (Any is now a pointer type in HIR):\n\n    struct Bag { payload: Any }\n    def main() {\n        let bag: Bag = Bag { payload: 7 }\n        let v: i64 = bag.payload   // reads the box's tag and size as a pointer\n    }\n\nFix: InsertValue consults `struct_carried_as_its_field` like every\nother site.","files":null}]}